  "What Makes The Perfect Business (5 Things)\nA Practical Playbook for Evaluating, Building, and Improving a Business\nIntroduction — The Five Advantages Behind a Strong Business\nA business can generate impressive revenue and still be difficult to own. It may need to replace every customer it loses, operate on thin margins, compete in a shrinking market, require constant reinvestment, or struggle to defend itself against competitors.",
  "A stronger business has structural advantages that make growth easier, cash flow more resilient, and the operation more attractive over time.",
  "This playbook organises those advantages into five characteristics:\n1. Sticky — customers continue buying, renewing, or spending more over time.\n2. Expensive — the business can charge substantially more than the direct cost of delivering its product or service, producing healthy gross margins.\n3. Expansion — the market or category is growing, creating a favourable demand environment.\n4. Air — the business has operational scale: low operational complexity, low capital requirements, or both.\n5. Unique — the business has a competitive moat that makes it difficult to copy, replace, or compete against purely on price.\nThe five characteristics are not an all-or-nothing checklist. Few businesses possess all of them at the highest level. They are advantages on a continuum. A business may be strong in one dimension and weak in another.",
  "The purpose of the framework is not to encourage an owner to abandon a functioning business because it does not score perfectly. It is to reveal the economic levers available to improve the business already in front of them—and to help a founder evaluate opportunities before committing years of effort.\nHow to use this playbook\n- If you are choosing a business: Use the five characteristics to compare opportunity vehicles.\n- If you already own a business: Diagnose its weakest characteristic and identify practical improvements.\n- If you are investing: Examine the quality and durability of the underlying economics, not only revenue growth.\n- If you are planning to scale: Understand how retention, margins, market growth, operational complexity, capital needs, and defensibility interact."
